                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input grid with black background (0) and blue squares (1). For each row, if two 1s have exactly one space (0) between them, fill that space with 2 (red). Only horizontal, not vertical.\n\nVerifier: x0 = leastcolor(I) -> the least common color. In example, 0 appears many times, 1 appears 6 times. leastcolor returns min by count, so 0 is least? Wait 0 count... grid 5x5=25 cells. 0 appears 19, 1 appears 6. Least common is 1 (count 6). So x0 = 1 = blue.\n\nx4 = rbind(ofcolor, x0) = ofcolor(grid, 1) -> indices of blue.\nx5 = chain(x3, delta, x4) where x3 = recolor(TWO). delta(ofcolor(I,1)) = backdrop minus patch = bounding box interior cells not blue. Then recolor 2 those cells.\n\nx2 = vsplit(I, x1) where x1 = height(I) = number of rows. vsplit with n = number of rows: h = len(grid)//n = 1, offset 0. So splits into n=height rows each of height 1. So each row treated separately.\n\nx6 = fork(paint, identity, x5) applied per row. For each row, ofcolor(row, 1), delta computes bounding box of blue cells in that row minus blue cells. If two blue separated, the bounding box includes the gap, so gap filled with 2. If only one blue, bounding box is single cell, no delta. If blue contiguous, no gap.\n\nNote: delta also would fill any non-blue cells within bounding box of blue cells. If row has 1 at columns 2 and 4, bbox 2-4, delta = column 3. Good. If row has 1 at 0,1,2? bbox 0-2 all blue, delta empty. If row has 1 at 0 and 3, delta = 1,2 both filled. Hmm, so if gap is more than one, all get filled.\n\nWait rule says \"single space in between\" but verifier fills entire bounding box between any blue cells in a row. Actually if there are multiple blues separated, bounding box spans min to max, filling everything between. So e.g. row 1,0,0,1 -> delta contains both zeros -> fills both. Let me check example row 4: [1,0,1,0,0] -> blue at 0,2, bbox 0-2, delta = col1 -> red at col1. Output row4: [1,2,1,0,0]. Yes.\n\nSo I need to crea